<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Akalkot State</h1><p class="st-meta">1708 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Akkalkot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bhonsle Maratha state of the Deccan, founded by the adopted son of Chhatrapati Shahu, and home of the saint Swami Samarth.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Akkalkot was granted in 1708 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehsinh-bhonsle">Fatehsinh Bhonsle</a>, born into the Lokhande family and adopted as a boy by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahu-i">Shahu</a>. His descendants ruled as Rajas under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapatis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Rajas governed a dry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solapur">Solapur</a>. After 1818 the state came under British protection, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-states-agency">Deccan States Agency</a>. In the 19th century Akkalkot became famous as the home of the saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-samarth">Swami Samarth</a>, whose shrine draws pilgrims.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Akkalkot merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bombay-presidency">Bombay Province</a> in 1948 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> in 1960, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solapur">Solapur</a> district.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/akalkot-state.jpg" alt="Akalkot State" width="320" height="175"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akalkot_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1708 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Akkalkot</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fatehsinh Bhonsle</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marathi, Kannada</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Akkalkot</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1828&amp;focus=akalkot-state" aria-label="Akalkot State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6750 1527 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Akalkot State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7070 1783L6991 1757M7070 1783L7364 1601M7070 1783L7364 1602M7070 1783L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6991 1757h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7070 1783h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7070 1783h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1828&amp;focus=akalkot-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
